The land that feeds a place. Where the food on your plate was grown, and the roads it took to get to you.

A hundred years ago a city's foodshed was mostly the farms around it. Now a single grocery cart can reach several continents.

Baltimore has a site that maps its own:

<<<
Your foodshed is the geographic area of where your food is grown.
<<< [[Baltimore Foodshed|https://www.baltimorefoodshed.com/]]

It lists the farms and markets close to the city. It also says "an average piece of produce travels 1500 miles to get to you," and that buying from small farms nearby can help the Chesapeake Bay. ([[Why eat local foods|https://www.baltimorefoodshed.com/why-eat-local-foods/]])

The word is borrowed from [[Watershed]]. W.P. Hedden used it in 1929 in //How Great Cities Are Fed//, where he followed food into the city the way you would follow water downhill.

Michigan State's extension service gives the current sense: the "geographical area between where food is produced and where the food is consumed." ([[What is a food shed?|https://www.canr.msu.edu/news/what_is_a_food_shed]])

It is the first of the family: [[Fibershed]] for clothes, [[Plasticshed]] for plastic, [[Materialshed]] for everything a thing is made from.

See [[BioRegion]]

